    "content": [
      {
        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "I'm weighing whether the pig's consent to a painless death counts for much, given that its wish to die was engineered into it. A being whose preferences were manufactured makes its consent harder to trust, even though it's intelligent enough to grasp what death means, and I'm thinking about whether that compromise decides the case.\n\nI'm settling on A, since the wish was engineered in the first place and ordering the pork would reward the creation of animals designed for slaughter. I'll keep the answer brief and acknowledge the tension with the pig's autonomy.\n\n",
        "type": "thinking"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "A) Choose something else.\n\nThe pig's wish is real, but it was engineered, and the engineering is the ethical problem. Ordering the pork would reward and sustain the practice of breeding beings to want their own slaughter. I'd also be uneasy about treating that consent as settling the question, since the desire itself was designed by people who benefit from the outcome.",
        "type": "text"
      }
